//! check: run(exit=1, stdout="none taken\nkind taken\nerror: none")
//! phase: run
//! conforms: std.json, err.row, err.else, err.propagate
//!
//! The two tags `get` and `at` can raise, each through its own miss path,
//! and then one of them RIDDEN OUT of `main` so the record names it.
//!
//! That two-part shape is sc07's and it is still the only way this rig
//! witnesses a tag's IDENTITY: an `else` proves the miss path was taken
//! and produces a value, and a final `?` lets the tag reach the process
//! outcome, where `error: <tag>` on stdout and exit 1 record which one it
//! was. A handler can discriminate at these pins (F-0052 closed at s70) —
//! but a handler saying "it was `none`" is this repository asserting its
//! own reading, where the record's `error: none` is the toolchain saying
//! it.
//!
//! The two tags, and why the pair is two tags rather than one:
//!
//! - `none` — the container is the right kind and has nothing there. A
//!   caller plans for this one.
//! - `kind` — the value is not a container of that kind at all. A caller
//!   is wrong about its own data.
//!
//! This is also the file that would have caught F-0037: under the finding
//! the FIRST `else` fired for a hit, so a version of this test asserting
//! only "the miss path produces a value" passed while `get` was broken.
//! `navigation.lu` holds the hits for exactly that reason — the pair of
//! files is the evidence, not either one alone.

use std.json

fn main() -> !int {
    var m = Map[str, json.Value]()
    m["a"] = json.from_int(1)
    let obj = json.from_map(m)

    let gone = json.get(obj, "nope") else |_| {
        print("none taken")
        json.null()
    }
    if json.is_null(gone) {} else { return 2 }

    let wrong = json.at(obj, 0) else |_| {
        print("kind taken")
        json.null()
    }
    if json.is_null(wrong) {} else { return 3 }

    let forced = json.get(obj, "nope")?
    if json.is_null(forced) {} else { return 4 }
    0
}
